   229	## 4. Chain II — two new representation-level gates
   230	
   231	Chain II is independent of Chain I: it never mentions `chi`, `nu`, or the
   232	normalisation of `E`; it uses Lemma F1 and 3-manifold topology, and it is
   233	decidable by a short permutation computation, which is what makes it
   234	usable as an instrument at every `N`.
   235	
   236	Throughout: `G = G_{p,q}`, `z = alpha^p` central, `rho : G -> S_N`
   237	transitive, `H = rho^{-1}(Stab_1)`, `Y = C^2 \ E` with `pi_1(Y) = H` and
   238	`H_1(Y) = Z^j` free on the meridians of the `j` components of `E`
   239	(Lemma F1, HT §2.1, HV confirmed). `rho(z)` is central in a transitive
   240	group, hence semiregular of order `kappa` with `M = N/kappa` orbits; those
   241	orbits are blocks of imprimitivity, `rho(z)` acts simply transitively on
   242	each, and the induced action on blocks factors through
   243	`bar G := G/<z> = Z/p * Z/q`, with block stabiliser `Delta` of index `M`.
   244	Write `bar m` for the induced permutation of `m = alpha^e beta^f`
   245	(`eq + fp = 1`) on the `M` blocks.
   246	
   247	`X = C^2 \ A_F` deformation-retracts onto the compact core
   248	`M_0 := S^3 \ int nu(T(p,q))` (weighted cone retraction, §2), so the
   249	covering `Y -> X` retracts onto the `N`-fold cover `Sigma_H -> M_0`:
   250	**`Y` is homotopy equivalent to a compact orientable 3-manifold `Sigma_H`
   251	whose boundary is `t` tori**, `t = #` orbits of the peripheral subgroup
   252	`P = <m, lambda>`; and `lambda = z m^{-pq}`, so
   253	`rho(P) = <rho(m), rho(z)>` and
   254	
   255	```text
   256	   t  =  # orbits of <rho(m), rho(z)> on N points  =  # cycles of bar m on M blocks .
   257	```
   258	
   259	`Sigma_H` is Seifert fibred over the degree-`M` orbifold cover
   260	`O'` of `D^2(p,q)`, and `t` is the number of boundary circles of `O'`,
   261	the `c` of the ORBIFOLD-CAGE derivation, so `2g + c = j` (HT §5.3) with
   262	`g = genus(O')`.
   263	
   264	### 4.1 THEOREM PERIPHERAL-RANK (the base orbifold has genus 0)
   265	
   266	> **THEOREM.** `t = c = j`, equivalently `g = 0`.
   267	>
   268	> *Proof.* Each meridian of a component `E_i` is a lift of `m` at a sheet
   269	> fixed by `rho(m)`, hence a loop in the preimage of the tube around
   270	> `A_F`, hence freely homotopic into `partial Sigma_H`. By Lemma F1 those
   271	> `j` meridians generate `H_1(Y) = H_1(Sigma_H)`. So
   272	> `H_1(partial Sigma_H; Q) -> H_1(Sigma_H; Q)` is **onto**. Half-lives-
   273	> half-dies for a compact orientable 3-manifold gives
   274	> `rank(image) = (1/2) dim H_1(partial Sigma_H; Q) = t`. Hence
   275	> `j = b_1(Sigma_H) = t`. With `2g + c = j` and `c = t = j`, `g = 0`. ∎
   276	
   277	This is strictly stronger than ORBIFOLD-CAGE `(C-1)`, which pins only the
   278	combination `2g + c = j`. As a gate it reads: **in the degree-`M` orbifold
   279	cover, `bar m` must have exactly `j` cycles.** MEASURED: all 32 `N = 8`
   280	records and the `N = 9` spot cell **pass** it (§5) — it is a real, cheap,
   281	all-`N` gate, but it is not what kills the survivors.
   282	
   283	### 4.2 THEOREM MERIDIAN-SPAN
   284	
   285	> **LEMMA (block conjugacy).** `Fix(rho(m))` is a union of `n_0 := a/kappa`
   286	> whole blocks, and for `i, i'` in the same block the meridian lifts
   287	> `h_i := t_i m t_i^{-1}` and `h_{i'}` are **conjugate in `H`**; hence
   288	> `[h_i] = [h_{i'}]` in `H^ab`.
   289	>
   290	> *Proof.* `rho(z)` commutes with `rho(m)`, so `Fix(rho(m))` is
   291	> `rho(z)`-invariant, i.e. a union of blocks; on a block `B`, `rho(m)`
   292	> commutes with the simply transitive `rho(z)|_B`, so `rho(m)|_B =
   293	> rho(z)^{k_B}|_B`, and a single fixed point forces `k_B = 0`, i.e.
   294	> `rho(m)|_B = id`. In particular `kappa | a` and `n_0 = a/kappa`. For
   295	> `i' = rho(z)^r(i)` we have `t_{i'} = h t_i z^r` for some `h in H`, and
   296	> `z` is central, so `h_{i'} = h (t_i m t_i^{-1}) h^{-1} = h h_i h^{-1}`. ∎
   297	
   298	> **THEOREM MERIDIAN-SPAN.** `kappa * j <= a`.
   299	>
   300	> *Proof.* The meridians of the `j` components of `E` are exactly the
   301	> classes `[h_i]`, `i in Fix(rho(m))` (a fixed sheet is a punctured
   302	> transverse disc centred at a point of `E`; meridians at different points
   303	> of the same irreducible component are conjugate, hence equal in `H_1`).
   304	> By Lemma F1 they are `j` **distinct** basis elements of `H^ab = Z^j`. By
   305	> the block-conjugacy lemma there are at most `n_0 = a/kappa` distinct
   306	> classes. So `j <= a/kappa`. ∎
   307	
   308	MEASURED, and this is the sharp check of the whole computation: the number
   309	of distinct meridian classes returned by the Reidemeister–Schreier driver
   310	equals `a/kappa` **exactly**, in 32/32 records (§9, control C4). The
   311	predicted values are `1` for the trefoil cell (`a = 2`, `kappa = 2`) and
   312	`2` for the family (`a = 4`, `kappa = 2`); the measured values are `1` and
   313	`2`. Against `j = 2` and `j = 3`: **FAIL**, uniformly.
   314	
   315	### 4.3 THEOREM CUSP-A-VOID-II
   316	
   317	> **THEOREM.** In case (A) no admissible `rho` exists at any `N >= 2`.
   318	>
   319	> *Proof.* `#cyc(bar m) >= #Fix(bar m) >= n_0` (every block on which
   320	> `rho(m)` is the identity is a fixed block). PERIPHERAL-RANK gives
   321	> `#cyc(bar m) = j`, so `j >= n_0 = a/kappa`, i.e. `a <= kappa j`.
   322	> MERIDIAN-SPAN gives `a >= kappa j`. Hence `a = kappa j` and the chain
   323	> `j = #cyc(bar m) = #Fix(bar m) = n_0` is an equality throughout.
   324	> `#cyc(bar m) = #Fix(bar m)` forces `bar m = id`, so `#Fix(bar m) = M`,
   325	> so `n_0 = M`: every block has `rho(m)|_B = id`, i.e. `rho(m) = id` and
   326	> `a = N`. But `W = N - a = sum_l s_l mu_l >= 2` (at least one dicritical,
   327	> `mu_l >= 2` by THEOREM 7.B' under `H2`). Contradiction. ∎
   328	
   329	Consumed here, at banked typing: Lemma F1 (HT, HV-confirmed);
   330	`2 <= j <= a <= N-2` (CUSP-KILL + `[P3]` + 7.B'); ORBIFOLD-CAGE's
   331	`2g + c = j`. Proved here: PERIPHERAL-RANK, the block-conjugacy lemma,
   332	MERIDIAN-SPAN. Chain II therefore does need `H2` (through 7.B'), whereas
   333	Chain I does not; that asymmetry is deliberate and is the reason both are
   334	reported.
   335	
   336	### 4.4 CUSP-A-KAPPA (charge item 2) — proved, by a stronger route
   337	
   338	SW §8 conjectures that every `kappa = 1` cage cell dies at the meridian
   339	cycle-type gate at every `N`. The conjecture is **true**, and PERIPHERAL-
   340	RANK alone proves it without any search:
   341	
   342	> **COROLLARY.** `kappa = 1` is impossible in case (A) at every `N >= 2`.
   343	>
   344	> *Proof.* `kappa = 1` iff `rho(z) = 1` (a central semiregular element with
   345	> a fixed point is trivial), so blocks are points, `M = N`, `bar m =
   346	> rho(m)`. PERIPHERAL-RANK: `j = #cyc(rho(m))`. CUSP-KILL: `j <= a =
   347	> #Fix(rho(m))`. Always `#Fix <= #cyc`. So
   348	> `j <= #Fix(rho(m)) <= #cyc(rho(m)) = j`: equality, hence every cycle of
   349	> `rho(m)` is a fixed point, `rho(m) = 1`, `a = N`, `W = 0`, against
   350	> `W >= 2`. ∎
   351	
   352	Stronger than conjectured in two ways: the obstruction is named exactly
   353	(the genus-0 identity, not the cycle type as such), and it is a proof
   354	rather than an exhaustion, so it also covers cells whose `alpha`-type
   355	splits into several conjugacy classes — the precise place where SW §8
   356	records that its `N = 9` conjugacy reduction does not run.
   357	
