    38	
    39	**Verdict headline.** The `H2` branch is *not* closed at all degrees. What is
    40	closed: `H3` (§2) and smooth `A_F` (§3) at every `N`; the whole of `N <= 3`;
    41	every profile whose singular points are all multibranch at `N = 4`; and, when
    42	every branch of `A_F` is smooth — which contains the whole nodal case — every
    43	`N <= 16`. The `N = 4` residual is pinned to one profile (§8). Typed verdict
    44	block: §10.
    45	
    46	## 1. Notation, the promoted dictionary, and the exact hypotheses of `(M')`
    47	
    48	### 1.1 The two pictures and the bijection between them
    49	
    50	Two constructions are in play and the whole lane depends on them agreeing.
    51	
    52	*Target-side (`Y`).* `Y := Spec B`, `B` the integral closure of `A = C[F_1,F_2]`
    53	in `K = C(x,y)`; `q : Y -> A^2` finite flat of degree `N`, `A^2 ≅ U ⊂ Y` open,
    54	`B_Y := Y \ U` pure of codimension one (sheet gate §2, review-CONFIRMED). For
    55	`p ∈ A^2`, `sum_{y ∈ q^{-1}(p)} e_y = N`. Components `B_1,...,B_m` of `B_Y`,
    56	`delta_j := deg(B_j -> D)`, `e_j := ord_{B_j}(q^*h)`.
    57	
    58	*Source-side (Orevkov).* `X ⊃ A^2` a smooth compactification on which
    59	`Phi : X -> P^2` is a morphism; dicriticals `l ⊂ X \ A^2` with affine image;
    60	`mu_l := ord_l(h ∘ F)`; `l' := l ∩ Phi^{-1}(A^2) ≅ A^1` (Orevkov Lemma 2.1,
    61	degree-free `[R1]`); `phi_l := Phi|_{l'} : A^1 -> D`, factoring as
    62	`phi_l = eta ∘ h_l` with `eta : D~ -> D` the normalization and
    63	`s_l := deg h_l`.
    64	
    65	> **Lemma 1.1 (dictionary).** Under `H2`, `j ↦ l` is a bijection between the
    66	> components of `B_Y` and the affine-image dicriticals of `F`, with
    67	> `e_j = mu_l` and `delta_j = s_l`.
    68	>
    69	> *Proof.* Let `l` be a dicritical with affine image. For `f ∈ A`, `Phi^*f` is
    70	> regular at the generic point of `l` because `Phi(l) = closure(D) ⊄ L_infty`;
    71	> so `ord_l >= 0` on `A`, hence on its integral closure `B`, so `ord_l` has a
    72	> centre on `Y`. That centre maps onto `D`, so it is a curve; it is not in
    73	> `U ≅ A^2` because `l` is a divisor at infinity of the source, so `ord_l` is
    74	> negative on some element of `C[x,y]`. So the centre is a component `B_j`, and
    75	> `ord_{B_j} = ord_l` as valuations of `K`. Conversely `B_j ⊄ U` makes
    76	> `ord_{B_j}` a valuation at infinity of the source, i.e. `ord_l` for a divisor
    77	> `l` on some `X`, dicritical with image `q(B_j) = D`. Equal valuations have
    78	> equal value groups and residue fields, so `e_j = ord_{B_j}(q^*h) =
    79	> ord_l(h∘F) = mu_l` and `delta_j = [kappa(B_j) : C(D)] = [C(l) : C(D)] = s_l`
    80	> (the last because `eta` is birational). ∎
    81	
    82	Consequences, all previously promoted and now with matching symbols:
    83	`N = a + sum_l s_l mu_l` `[P3]`; `W := N - a = sum_l s_l mu_l >= 2`, so
    84	`1 <= a <= N - 2` (sheet gate Prop 2.4, review-CONFIRMED at exactly this scope);
    85	`b := sum_{mu_l = 1} s_l`. **THEOREM 7.B** says no affine-image dicritical has
    86	`mu_l = 1`; under `H2` every dicritical has affine image `= D`, so
    87	
    88	> **(7.B')  every `mu_l >= 2`; hence `b = 0`, `m >= 1`, and `2 sum_l s_l <= W`.**
    89	
    90	### 1.2 What `(M')` needs, precisely
    91	
    92	`(M')` is
    93	`a(nu + s - 1) - sum_{p ∈ Sing D} a_p = N nu - 1` with `1 <= a <= N-2`,
    94	`0 <= a_p <= a`; equivalently the defect form
    95	`sum_p (a - a_p) = (a-1) + nu(N-a)`. Its proof (sheet gate §7, review §6:
    96	"Every substitution checks") uses exactly three ingredients: Theorem (E)
    97	`1 = N(1 - chi_c(D)) + chi_c(F^{-1}(D))` (Keller only); the covering lemma
    98	`F^{-1}(D_0) -> D_0` of degree `a` (Keller + `H2`); and
    99	`chi_c(D) = 1 - nu`, which is **`H3`** — the statement that the normalization
   100	`D~` is `A^1`.
   101	
   102	`H3` is therefore load-bearing, and it is the first thing this lane has to
   103	secure. The promoted derivation of `H3` (`[P5]`, `b0-all-n` Thm 4.1(1)) has the
   104	hypothesis *"some dicritical has `mu_0 = 1`"* — which THEOREM 7.B refutes. The
   105	implication survives 7.B only vacuously; it does **not** deliver `H3`. §2 fixes
   106	this, and the fix is cheaper than the original.
   107	
   108	For the record I also carry the `H3`-free form. Writing `theta` for the number
   109	of places of `D` at infinity, `chi_c(D~) = 2 - theta - 2g` for `D~` of genus
   110	`g`, and the same assembly gives
   111	
   112	> **(M'_theta)** `sum_p (a - a_p) = (a-1) + T(N-a)`, `T := nu + theta + 2g - 1`.
   113	
   114	Since `theta >= 1` and `g >= 0`, `T >= nu`, so relaxing `H3` only **raises** the
   115	right-hand side: every kill below is a fortiori valid without `H3`. §2 shows
   116	`T = nu` anyway.
   117	
   118	## 2. LEMMA A — `H3` is a theorem under `H2` (repair of a vacuous promotion)
   119	
   120	> **LEMMA A.** Let `F` be a noninvertible plane Keller map and `D_l` any
   121	> irreducible component of `A_F` that is the image of a dicritical. Then the
   122	> projective model of `D_l` is rational, `D_l` has exactly **one** place at
   123	> infinity, and `D~_l ≅ A^1`. Under `H2` this is `H3`, and it needs **no**
   124	> trivial dicritical.
   125	>
   126	> *Proof.* Fix a dicritical `l` with `Phi(l) = closure(D_l)`. By Orevkov Lemma
   127	> 2.1 (`[R1]`, degree-free, re-derived from `refs/jc86.pdf` p. 2 in N20 and
   128	> review-CONFIRMED "without H2 or H3"), `l ≅ P^1` and `l' := l ∩ Phi^{-1}(A^2)`
   129	> is `l` minus a single point, so `l' ≅ A^1`. `Phi|_l : P^1 -> closure(D_l)` is
   130	> a finite surjective morphism of projective curves, and
   131	> `l' = (Phi|_l)^{-1}(D_l)`, so `phi_l := Phi|_{l'} : A^1 -> D_l` is finite and
   132	> surjective. Factor it through the normalization, `phi_l = eta_l ∘ h_l` with
   133	> `h_l : A^1 -> D~_l` finite surjective of degree `s_l`.
   134	>
   135	> (i) *Rationality.* `h_l` extends to a surjection `h̄_l : P^1 -> \bar D~_l` of
   136	> smooth projective curves, so `\bar D~_l` is rational by Lüroth; `g = 0`.
   137	>
   138	> (ii) *One place at infinity.* `D~_l` is affine (finite over the affine `D_l`),
   139	> so `S := \bar D~_l \ D~_l ≠ ∅`. `h_l(A^1) ⊆ D~_l`, so
   140	> `h̄_l^{-1}(S) ⊆ {∞}`. `h̄_l` is surjective, so every point of `S` has nonempty
   141	> preimage inside the one-point set `{∞}`: `#S = 1`.
   142	>
   143	> So `D~_l ≅ P^1 \ {1 point} ≅ A^1`. ∎
   144	
   145	Three remarks, because this is a repair of a promoted item and must not be
   146	oversold.
   147	
   148	1. **What is new.** The promoted route (`[P5]`; `b0-all-n` §2.3,
   149	   review-CONFIRMED) reaches `H3` by Riemann–Hurwitz on `h̄_0` *after* using
   150	   `mu_0 = 1` to force `h_0` étale, with extra output `s_0 = 1`. Lemma A drops
   151	   `s_l = 1` — which nothing downstream of 7.B needs — and buys `H3` from Lüroth
   152	   plus a one-line covering argument: weaker conclusion, weaker hypothesis.
   153	2. **Why the repair is necessary.** After 7.B no dicritical has `mu = 1`, so
   154	   `[P5]`'s antecedent is empty and its consequent is not asserted; the
   155	   coordinator's bullet "`H3` as a conclusion" is, read against `[P5]`, a
   156	   vacuous implication. Every consumer of `(M')` needs `H3` as a *fact*.
   157	3. **Scope.** Lemma A is componentwise and needs neither `H2` nor `H3`, so
   158	   `chi_c(D_i) = 1 - nu_i` holds componentwise in the reducible branch too — a
   159	   free sharpening flagged for those lanes but **not** used below.
   160	
   161	Consequences used from here on, all now unconditional under `H2`:
   162	`D~ ≅ A^1`; `chi_c(D) = 1 - nu`; `chi_c(D_0) = 1 - nu - s`; `T = nu` in
   163	`(M'_theta)`, so `(M')` and `(M'-def)` hold verbatim; each `h_l : A^1 -> A^1` is
   164	a degree-`s_l` polynomial map, totally ramified at `∞`, so by Riemann–Hurwitz
   165	
   166	> **(RH_l)  `sum_{t ∈ A^1} (e_t - 1) = s_l - 1`**, hence `h_l` has at most
   167	> `s_l - 1` finite critical points; write `R := sum_l (s_l - 1)`.
   168	
   169	## 3. THEOREM SMOOTH-KILL — `A_F` is singular at every degree
   170	
   171	REP-96 §7 (R4) runs the nodal collapse at `N = 4` in the two branches `a = 2`
   172	and `a = 1`, and the `a = 1` branch is refuted "for every `s >= 1`". The
   173	boundary case `s = 0` is therefore *not* covered: a nodal curve with no nodes is
   174	a smooth curve, and `(M')` is **satisfied** there. This is the first thing a
   175	general-`N` treatment has to close, and it closes completely.
   176	
   177	> **THEOREM SMOOTH-KILL.** Let `F` be a noninvertible plane Keller map with
   178	> `A_F` irreducible. Then `A_F` is **singular**: `s >= 1`.
   179	>
   180	> *Proof.* Suppose `s = 0`. Then `nu = 0` and `D = D_0` is smooth, irreducible,
   181	> with `D~ ≅ A^1` (Lemma A); a smooth curve equals its normalization, so
   182	> `D ≅ A^1` and `chi_c(D) = 1`.
   183	>
   184	> (a) *`a = 1`.* Lemma 3.3 makes `E := F^{-1}(D) -> D` a covering of degree `a`,
   185	> so `chi_c(E) = a·chi_c(D) = a`. Theorem (E) gives
   186	> `1 = N(1 - chi_c(D)) + chi_c(E) = N·0 + a`. Hence `a = 1`.
   187	>
   188	> (b) *Both curves are coordinate lines.* `D ⊂ A^2` is closed and isomorphic to
   189	> `A^1`, so by **Abhyankar–Moh–Suzuki** there is `α ∈ Aut(A^2)` with
   190	> `α(D) = {v = 0}`. Replace `F` by `α ∘ F`: still Keller, still noninvertible,
   191	> and `A_{α∘F} = α(A_F) = {v = 0}`. Now `a = 1` makes `E -> D` an étale map of
   192	> degree one, i.e. an isomorphism, so `E ≅ A^1`; `E` is closed in `A^2`, so AMS
   193	> again gives `β ∈ Aut(A^2)` with `β^{-1}(E) = {x = 0}`. Replace `F` by `F ∘ β`.
   194	>
   195	> (c) *Contradiction.* `D = {v = 0}` has reduced equation `h = v`, so
   196	> `h ∘ F = F_2` and `F^*(D) = div(F_2)`. `F` is étale, so `F^*(D)` is reduced
   197	> (sheet gate Cor 3.4): `F_2` is square-free with zero set `E = {x = 0}`, i.e.
   198	> `F_2 = c·x`, `c ∈ C^*` (units of `C[x,y]` are constants). Then
   199	> `Jac F = -c·∂F_1/∂y ∈ C^*`, so `∂F_1/∂y = λ ∈ C^*` and
   200	> `F_1 = λ y + g(x)`. Then `F = (λ y + g(x), c x)` is an automorphism —
   201	> contradicting noninvertibility. ∎
   202	
   203	Three notes.
   204	
   205	* **Hypotheses used**: Keller; `H2` (only through Lemma A); Theorem (E) (Keller
   206	  only); Lemma 3.3 and Cor 3.4; AMS (char 0). No `(M')`, no 7.B, no budget.
   207	* **It is not the promoted "smooth-`A_F` law"**, which states *`A_F` smooth ⟹
   208	  `a = 1`* and stops there (step (a) here). I did not find this closure in the
   209	  campaign record; typed PROVED-HERE, UNREVIEWED.
   210	* **Corollary.** Everything below may assume `s >= 1`; the `a = 1`, `s = 0`
   211	  escape REP-96 §7 (R4) leaves open at `N = 4` is closed at every degree.
   212	
   213	## 4. The local law `[P7]`, re-derived, and the master defect identity
   214	
   215	`(M')` is a *global* identity; every kill below comes from its **pointwise
   216	refinement**, which the campaign has as `[P7]` (`ALL-N (4.5)`, review §4.3
   217	CONFIRMED). Because `[P7]` is quoted in `N20` inside a `[P5]`-flavoured context
   218	(`r_p >= 2` there comes from `[P5]`), I re-derive it, to fix that the identity
   219	itself is `[P5]`-free. One step of the promoted derivation is reused verbatim
   220	and flagged inside the proof.
   221	
   222	> **LEMMA 4.1 (fibre law).** For every `p ∈ D`,
   223	> `N - a_p = r_p·W + K_p` with `K_p := sum_l sum_{t ∈ h_l^{-1}(eta^{-1}(p))} k_t
   224	> >= 0`, where `k_t := mu_t - e_t mu_l >= 0` (`[P8]`/`ALL-N (4.3)`, from Orevkov
   225	> Lemma 3.1) and `e_t` is the local degree of `h_l` at `t`.
   226	>
   227	> *Proof.* Fix `x` near `p`, `x ∉ D`. `F` is proper of degree `N` over
   228	> `A^2 \ D`, so `#F^{-1}(x) = N`. Of these, exactly `a_p` stay near
   229	> `F^{-1}(p) ∩ A^2` (`F` étale: each of the `a_p` points has exactly one
   230	> preimage of `x` in a small neighbourhood). The remaining `N - a_p` escape,
   231	> i.e. accumulate on `X \ A^2`. By Orevkov Lemma 2.1 the boundary is a disjoint
   232	> union of linear chains, each with a **single** `L_F` endpoint `l` (the
   233	> dicritical) and a contracted `L_C` tail that lands at one attachment point of
   234	> `l`; so escaping preimages are indexed by points of `l'`, and `mu_t` — the
   235	> local multiplicity of Orevkov's contracted model at `t`, whose generic value
   236	> is `mu_l` by the Lemma 3.1 normal form `u = x'`, `v = y'^{mu_l}` — is exactly
   237	> the number escaping into the chain attached at `t`. **The contracted tail is
   238	> not an extra source of fibre points: its contribution is what `mu_t` counts**
   239	> (`ALL-N`, "no extra finite `L_C`-fibre points", review §4.3 CONFIRMED); this
   240	> is the one step I take from the promoted derivation rather than rebuild.
   241	> Distinct chains meet only in `L_infty`, so no `t` is counted twice. Hence
   242	> `N - a_p = sum_l sum_{t ∈ phi_l^{-1}(p)} mu_t`. Now
   243	> `phi_l^{-1}(p) = h_l^{-1}(eta^{-1}(p))` and `#eta^{-1}(p) = r_p`; for each of
   244	> those `r_p` places `z`, `sum_{t ∈ h_l^{-1}(z)} e_t = s_l` (degree of a finite
   245	> map of smooth curves). So
   246	> `sum_{t} mu_t = sum_l (mu_l · r_p · s_l) + K_p = r_p W + K_p`. ∎
   247	
   248	At `p ∈ D_0` (`r_p = 1`, `K_p = 0`) this is `N = a + W` — the promoted `[P3]`,
   249	a positive control. Subtracting from `N = a + W` gives the form used throughout:
   250	
   251	> **(L)  `a - a_p = (r_p - 1)·W + K_p`,  `K_p >= 0`,  `a_p >= 0`.**
   252	
   253	Summing `(L)` over `Sing D` and comparing with `(M'-def)`
   254	`sum_p (a - a_p) = (a-1) + nu W` gives, with `nu = sum_p (r_p - 1)`,
   255	
   256	> **(K)  `sum_{p ∈ Sing D} K_p = a - 1`.**
   257	
   258	`(K)` is `ALL-N (4.6)`; note it is *equivalent* to `(M')` given `(L)`, so `(M')`
   259	is not an extra input once `(L)` is available — and it also equals Orevkov's
   260	`sum_l (mu_l + corr_l) = N - 1` after substituting
   261	`corr_l = mu_l(s_l - 1) + sum_t k_t` and `(RH_l)`. Three independent routes,
   262	one answer: a positive control on the whole apparatus.
   263	
   264	Two immediate structural consequences of `a_p >= 0` in `(L)`:
   265	
   266	> **(C1) `r_p·W <= N` for every `p ∈ Sing D`.** In particular if some `p` has
   267	> `r_p >= 2` then `2(N - a) <= N`, i.e. **`2a >= N`**, and then `K_p <= N - 2W =
   268	> 2a - N =: D_gap` at every multibranch `p`.
   269	>
   270	> **(C2)** If `p` is unibranch (`r_p = 1`) then `a - a_p = K_p`, and `a_p >= 0`
   271	> gives only `K_p <= a`. **The `D_gap` ceiling is available exactly at
   272	> multibranch points.** This single asymmetry is what separates the nodal case
   273	> from the cuspidal one; it is not an artefact of the bookkeeping.
   274	
   275	Finally, two localization lemmas, in both directions. Write `NI` for the set of
   276	places `z ∈ D~` with `d eta_z = 0` — equivalently, places whose branch of `D` is
   277	a **singular** branch.
   278	
   279	> **LEMMA 4.2 (forward localization).** If `k_t > 0` then either `e_t >= 2` (so
   280	> `t` is a critical point of `h_l`) or `h_l(t) ∈ NI`.
   281	> *Proof.* If `e_t = 1` then `k_t = mu_t - mu_l > 0`, so `d phi_l(t) = 0` by
   282	> `[P4]` (`[Z-6.5b]`), and `d phi_l(t) = d eta_{h_l(t)} ∘ d h_l(t)` with
   283	> `d h_l(t) ≠ 0`; hence `d eta_{h_l(t)} = 0`. ∎
   284	>
   285	> **LEMMA 4.3 (reverse localization).** If `z ∈ NI` lies over `p` and some
   286	> dicritical `l` has a point `t ∈ h_l^{-1}(z)` with `e_t = 1`, then `K_p >= 1`.
   287	> In particular this holds whenever some `s_l = 1`.
   288	> *Proof.* `d phi_l(t) = d eta_z ∘ d h_l(t) = 0`, so `mu_t > mu_l` by `[P4]`,
   289	> and `k_t = mu_t - e_t mu_l = mu_t - mu_l >= 1`. ∎
   290	
   291	Writing `beta := #{p ∈ Sing D : some branch of D at p is singular}` and using
   292	`(RH_l)`, Lemma 4.2 gives the counting bound this lane runs on:
   293	
   294	> **(C3)  `#{p ∈ Sing D : K_p > 0} <= R + beta`,  `R = sum_l (s_l - 1)`.**
   295	
   296	## 5. Task (1): the nodal collapse at general `d = N`
   297	
   298	### 5.1 The crude collapse, and exactly how far it goes
   299	
   300	"Nodal" in the charge means `nu = s`, i.e. **every singular point has exactly
   301	two branches**. (It therefore also covers tacnodes, ordinary tangencies and all
   302	`A_{2k+1}`; I flag below where genuine smoothness of the branches is needed.)
   303	With `nu = s`, `(M'-def)` is
   304	
   305	```text
   306	(5.1)   sum_{p ∈ Sing A_F} (a - a_p) = (a - 1) + s(N - a),   0 <= a_p <= a.
   307	```
   308	
   309	`LHS <= s·a`, so a solution needs `s·a >= (a-1) + s(N-a)`, i.e.
   310	
   311	```text
   312	(5.2)   s·(2a - N) >= a - 1.
   313	```
   314	
   315	Exact case analysis, `1 <= a <= N-2`, `N >= 3`, and `s >= 1` by SMOOTH-KILL:
   316	
   317	* `2a <= N`. If `a >= 2` then `LHS <= 0 < a-1 = RHS`: impossible. If `a = 1`
   318	  then `(5.2)` reads `s(2 - N) >= 0`, i.e. `s(N-2) <= 0`; `N >= 3` forces
   319	  `s = 0`, contradicting SMOOTH-KILL. **No solution.**
   320	* `2a > N`. `(5.2)` holds for `s >= (a-1)/(2a-N)`, so solutions exist
   321	  numerically. But `a <= N-2` and `2a > N` require `N/2 < a <= N-2`, hence
   322	  `N > 4`.
   323	
   324	> **PROPOSITION 5.1.** Under `H2`, with at least one multibranch point:
   325	> (i) at `N = 3` this is impossible (`(C1)` gives `2a >= 3` while
   326	> `a <= N-2 = 1`); (ii) at `N = 4` it forces `a = 2`, `W = 2`, `D_gap = 0`, so
   327	> `K_p = 0` at every multibranch `p`, and `(K)` puts the whole excess
   328	> `a - 1 = 1` on **unibranch** points: `A_F` must have a **cusp**. In
   329	> particular there is no nodal `A_F` at `N ∈ {3,4}`, and more generally no
   330	> `A_F` all of whose singular points are multibranch.
   331	
   332	Proposition 5.1 subsumes REP-96 §7 (R4) at `N = 4`: R4 kills nodal `A_F` there
   333	for `s >= 1`; SMOOTH-KILL closes `s = 0`; `(C1)` upgrades "nodal" to
   334	"every singular point multibranch". The `a = d-2` boundary is not hand-waved: at
   335	`N = 4` it is `a = 2` and dies because `s(2a-N) = 0 < a-1 = 1`; at general `N`
   336	it dies inside the `2a > N` branch below. But `LHS <= s·a` is **not** enough for `N >= 5` — the
   337	`2a > N` branch is genuinely non-empty for `(5.2)` — and the sharpening comes
   338	from `(L)`, `(K)`, `(C3)` and 7.B.
   339	
   340	### 5.2 The sharp form: all branches smooth
   341	
   342	Assume every branch of `A_F` is smooth. Then a singular point cannot be
   343	unibranch, so `r_p >= 2` for all `p ∈ Sing D`, `beta = 0`, and `nu >= s` (nodal
   344	is the case `nu = s`). By `(C1)`, `K_p <= D_gap := 2a - N` for every `p`; by
   345	`(C3)`, at most `R` points have `K_p > 0`; by `(K)` their total is `a - 1`. So
   346	
   347	```text
   348	(5.3)   a - 1 <= R · D_gap ,     D_gap = 2a - N,   R = sum_l (s_l - 1).
   349	```
   350	
   351	If `a = 1` then `(K)` is `0 = 0`, but `(C1)` gives `2 >= 2a >= N >= 3`:
   352	impossible. So `a >= 2`, `D_gap >= 1`. Now 7.B enters through `R`: since every
   353	`mu_l >= 2` and `sum_l s_l mu_l = W`, we have `sum_l s_l <= floor(W/2)` and
   354	`R = sum_l s_l - m <= floor(W/2) - 1` (`m >= 1`). Writing `R_max(W)` for the
   355	exact maximum of `R` over dicritical profiles of weight `W` with all `mu_l >= 2`
   356	(`= W/2 - 1` for `W` even; `max(W/p_min(W) - 1, (W-5)/2)` for `W` odd), the
   357	system to solve is
   358	
   359	```text
   360	(5.4)   a - 1 <= (2a - N)·R_max(N - a),    N/2 < a <= N - 2,   N - a >= 2.
   361	```
   362	
   363	Using the clean bound `R <= W/2 - 1` and `a = (N + D_gap)/2`, `W = (N-D_gap)/2`,
   364	`(5.4)` becomes `2(N + D_gap - 2) <= D_gap(N - D_gap - 4)`, i.e.
   365	
   366	```text
   367	(5.5)   N·(D_gap - 2) >= D_gap^2 + 6 D_gap - 4.
   368	```
   369	
   370	`D_gap = 1` gives `-N >= 11`, `D_gap = 2` gives `0 >= 12`: both impossible, so
   371	`D_gap >= 3`; then `D_gap = 3 ⟹ N >= 23`, `4 ⟹ N >= 18`, `5 ⟹ N >= 17`,
   372	`6 ⟹ N >= 17`, `7,8 ⟹ N >= 18`, and the right side of `(5.5)` divided by
   373	`D_gap - 2` is increasing for `D_gap >= 6`. Parity (`D_gap ≡ N mod 2`) removes
   374	`D_gap = 6` at `N = 17`.
   375	
   376	> **THEOREM NODAL-ALL-N.** Let `F` be a noninvertible plane Keller map of
   377	> geometric degree `N` with `A_F` irreducible, and suppose every branch of
   378	> `A_F` is smooth (in particular, `A_F` nodal). Then **`N >= 17`**, and the
   379	> admissible data are exactly the solutions of `(5.4)`. At `N = 17` the unique
   380	> solution saturates both inequalities:
   381	> `a = 11, W = 6, D_gap = 5, m = 1, (s_1,mu_1) = (3,2), R = 2`, with
   382	> `sum_p K_p = 10` concentrated as `K = 5` at exactly two singular points, each
   383	> then having `r_p = 2` and `a_p = 0`; every other singular point has `r_p = 2`,
   384	> `K_p = 0`, `a_p = 5`; `s >= 2`.
   385	
   386	The first admissible `(N, a, W, D_gap, R_max)` tuples, by exhaustive integer
   387	enumeration of `(5.4)`:
   388	
   389	```text
   390	N=17: (11, 6, 5,2)          N=20: (12, 8,4,3) (14,6, 8,2)
   391	N=18: (12, 6, 6,2)          N=21: (13, 8,5,3) (15,6, 9,2)
   392	N=19: (13, 6, 7,2)          N=22: (14, 8,6,3) (16,6,10,2)
   393	                            N=23: (13,10,3,4) (15,8,7,3) (17,6,11,2)
   394	```
   395	
   396	**Robustness.** If THEOREM 7.B were withdrawn (allowing `mu_l = 1`, i.e.
   397	`b > 0`), `R` could reach `W - 1` and the same enumeration gives only `N >= 8`
   398	(first survivor `a=5, W=3, D_gap=2, R=2`). So 7.B carries exactly the range
   399	`8 <= N <= 16`, and the `N <= 7` part of THEOREM NODAL-ALL-N is 7.B-free.
