   653	## 9. Task (3): typed OPENs and the cheapest decisive instrument for each
   654	
   655	Four survivors. Each is named with its exact data and its cheapest decisive
   656	instrument from the campaign's current toolset.
   657	
   658	```text
   659	OPEN[MPRIME-B1-N>=17]
   660	```
   661	Case (B1) at `N >= 17`: every branch of `A_F` smooth, data solving `(5.4)`; the
   662	`N = 17` cell is fully rigid (§5.2). **Instrument:** not a computation — the
   663	cheapest kill is an *upper* bound on `R = sum_l (s_l - 1)`, i.e. on the total
   664	finite ramification of the dicritical covers `h_l : A^1 -> A^1`. `R <= 1`
   665	would give `a - 1 <= D_gap = 2a - N`, i.e. `a >= N - 1`, against `a <= N - 2`:
   666	**the whole of (B1) dies at every `N` as soon as `R <= 1` is proved**
   667	(`R = 0` gives `a = 1`, already excluded in case (B)). `R = 0`
   668	is exactly "every dicritical is birational onto `A_F`"; `R <= 1` is one step
   669	weaker. This is a valuation-theoretic statement about a single polynomial map
   670	and is the highest-value successor in this lane.
