    95	### 2.1 (F1) Gysin freeness — proved, not cited
    96	
    97	> **LEMMA F1.** Let `E ⊂ C^2` be a curve with `r` irreducible components.
    98	> Then `H_1(C^2 \ E; Z) ≅ Z^r`, free, with the meridians as a basis.
    99	>
   100	> *Proof.* Let `f_1,...,f_r` be reduced defining polynomials of the
   101	> components. `H_1(C^2\E;Z)` is generated by meridians `mu_1,...,mu_r`
   102	> (Zariski–van Kampen: the group is generated by meridians of the
   103	> components, and conjugate meridians agree in `H_1`). The winding-number
   104	> homomorphism `w = (deg f_1,...,deg f_r) : H_1(C^2\E) -> Z^r`, induced
   105	> by `(f_1,...,f_r) : C^2\E -> (C^*)^r`, sends `mu_i` to the `i`-th basis
   106	> vector. A generating set mapping to a basis under a homomorphism to a
   107	> free module is itself a basis. ∎
   108	
   109	This is the exact statement ID §3.2 uses and the exact statement MPRIME
   110	§7 consumes inside CUSP-CAGE (`H^ab = Z^j` torsion-free). It needs no
   111	Libgober, no characteristic variety, and no `P^2` bookkeeping. Recorded
