./xmodel/cusp-a-n8-gate-opus5-20260902.log:8:**The datum the charge had wrong.** The charge states "E has exactly `a` singular points, each analytically a (p,q) cusp." It has none. `a = #Fix(ρ(m))` is the *generic*-fibre count; at the cusp point `c` the local group is the *whole* π₁ (because `{x^p=y^q}` is a cone, so `π₁(B_c∖A_F) ≅ π₁(C²∖A_F)`), so a preimage of `c` would be a section of a connected degree-N covering. Hence `F⁻¹(c) = ∅` and **E is smooth**. That single correction is what makes the Euler gate lethal — on the charge's premise it would have been free.
./xmodel/cusp-a-n8-gate-opus5-20260902.log:10:**Chain I (Euler, H2-free).** χ(E) = χ(C²) − N·χ(C²∖A_F) = 1; E smooth ⟹ components disjoint ⟹ some `E_i` has χ = 1, i.e. `E_i ≅ A¹`; but `E_i → A_F∖{c} ≅ C*` is non-constant, and there is no non-constant unit in `C[t]`.
