./xmodel/sol-ucda.log:3780:./APPROACHES.md:165:Fable's input list (verbatim source for F1–F18, delivered with the merge charge): 1 Newton-polygon corner-cutting (GGV-Horruitiner); 2 Sheet-number ladder (Sigray frame); 3 Valuation theory/dicritical divisors (Abhyankar, Favre–Jonsson); 4 Links at infinity/splice diagrams (Neumann, Cassou-Nogues, Orevkov); 5 Open-surface/log-Kodaira (Miyanishi, Kaliman); 6 Mathieu subspaces/Zhao image conjectures; 7 Dixmier route: attack End(A_1) directly (Bavula program); 8 Reduction mod p/p-curvature (Tsuchimoto); 9 Formal-inverse combinatorics (BCW tree formula, Wright, Singer); 10 Aut(A^2) structure/polydegree (Jung–van der Kulk, Furter); 11 Nonproperness/asymptotic variety (Jelonek); 12 Monodromy/Galois of generic fiber (Razar, Wright); 13 Ax-Grothendieck/injectivity route (Cynk–Rusek); 14 Analytic/dynamical (Fatou–Bieberbach, Pinchuk constraint); 15 Guided counterexample search beyond frontier; 16 Char-p analogue lifting; 17 Formal-germ certification + algebraization (DEPTH-STAB/Newton/e+); 18 D-module/holonomic index.
./xmodel/sol-ucda.log:3785:./xmodel/sol-probabilities-0821.md:19:**Estimate: 5% by 2031-08-21 (a five-year horizon).** I count success only as an expert-checkable characteristic-zero proof of JC2 or an explicit characteristic-zero polynomial Keller counterexample. “Current avenues” means the named programs in this snapshot and their direct technical descendants—sheet-book/FC closure, the D43/D75/H29 residue-A experiments, PCC/WTC/log-ramification, Euler-characteristic/nonproperness, DC2, and the off-axis/formal-construction work—pursued at roughly comparable intensity; I exclude a conceptually unrelated future breakthrough merely adopted by the campaign. The positive case is genuine: repeated computational cliffs have yielded small exact certificates, D25 gives 576 disjoint affine 14-cells at each tested prime, and the radical-fiber symmetry is proved. But D25 survived rather than died, no germ was certified in the sampled cells, D23 and D25 used the same depth window, and D43 is only the first new operator measurement while still consuming conjectural x-side and registry inputs. The proof routes lack a complete global landing or absolute degree bound; the counterexample route must still cross modular-to-formal, characteristic-zero, algebraization, globalization, and polynomiality gaps. Calibrationally, I assign about 6% campaign success conditional on JC2 being true and about 1% conditional on it being false; using the 70/30 truth split gives 4.5%, rounded to 5%.
./xmodel/sol-ucda.log:3787:./xmodel/sol-probabilities-0821.md:25:**Estimate: 90%.** I interpret “solution exists” in a horizon-free, proof-object sense: there is a finite, independently checkable resolution in ordinary characteristic-zero mathematics—a proof if JC2 is true or an explicit polynomial counterexample if it is false—whose indispensable dependency graph does **not** include exhaustive construction, termination, or a verdict for the particular residue-A, B-frozen, no-log, PIN42, `W1W2 != 0` cell tower at unbounded depth. The solution may reuse general lemmas discovered while studying that tower; its verification may not require the tower verdict. This is not the probability that the campaign finds such a solution soon. Residue A is a narrow modular chart, and no theorem in the repository makes it the universal endpoint of every counterexample or forces global approaches through it. The repository itself contains logically different candidates—global ramification/concentration, Euler-characteristic and nonproperness arguments, and DC2. Conditional on JC2 being true, I put about 86% on some finite non-tower proof existing; conditional on JC2 being false, I put about 99% on a directly verifiable counterexample not requiring the discovery tower. With the 70/30 truth split, that is approximately 90% unconditionally.
./xmodel/sol-ucda.log:3821:./xmodel/grok-approaches.md:140:**(b) Tried as a consistency check, not as a construction of \(A(F)\).** `SHEET6-CLASSICAL.md` T4: geometric degree \(\mu(F)=6\), non-properness excess \(\ge 6\), *passes*. `AM-CHECK.md` names the A(F) route and leaves it “not run here.” `SOL-PROP58.md` uses Chau’s theorem on exceptional-value curves to kill a specialization defect — that is adjacent (exceptional values, not the full asymptotic set).
./xmodel/sol-ucda.log:3822:./xmodel/grok-approaches.md:142:**(c) Stuck.** Describing \(A(F)\) for a general Keller map is historically as hard as JC2 (Jelonek, Vitushkin, Kaliman). The campaign computed numerical constraints *assuming* the residue-A genome, which any actual pair in that template would have to obey — a consistency check, not a non-existence proof. The missing object is an equation for \(A(F)\) from \(J=1\) and the pinned Newton data, or a contradiction between AM on \(A(F)\) components and the forced pole orders. Field obstruction: \(A(F)\) is defined from the map at infinity; computing it is the compactification problem again.
